Typesetting notes -- DRAFT, NOT RULED (2026-11-03)
    Tony and Clay's sketch, as relayed by Clay
        Three jobs: describe (markup), lay out (break and place), render
        (backend).
        Drawing is absolute placement (draw here); typesetting is flowing
        placement (wherever it fits next). Both sit on one thin C++ floor:
        measure text, draw glyphs and lines.
        Take TeX's engine, not its language: boxes and glue, whole-paragraph
        line breaking, flow into frames. Kant rules and actions replace TeX's
        macro expansion.
        Forms are half of it already: down / across are box lists, a valueless
        space is glue, fit is shrink-wrap.
        Two trees: a source tree (the what: document, paragraphs, runs) is
        poured into a frame tree (the where: form fields). Test: if it can
        break across frames it is flow; a fixed place makes it a form field.
        Emphasis: marked text becomes its own run; its style is its parent's
        plus a named change, scoped to the run. Markup names the meaning; the
        framing style says what it looks like. A wholesale named style is the
        case where the change sets everything.
        Prose is written in the typesetting language: text by default,
        commands as the exception. noSkip ^ and dtext^= are prior art.
        print source to frame becomes the pour command.

    Clod's reply
        Text by default: fits as a prose MODE, not a grammar-wide default.
        The closer prior art is the (...#) literal -- already text by default
        with one exit. Where it hurts: every rule skips leading space unless
        ^, so the whole prose path must be noSkip; checkSkip's indent
        adoption would read an indented paragraph as nesting; comments are
        parsed, not skipped (bear-traps 4, 29, 53), so // in a URL bites;
        whitespace-only text has no data (bear-trap 28), so spaces must be
        glue nodes, never string runs; and a failed parse abandons the rest
        of the input at exit 0 -- for a document that is a silently lost
        chapter, so prose mode must fail loudly or carry an end sentinel.
        Pick one rare escape character; # is taken.
        The pour and the Buffer mark: the live Buffer has ONE mark (Frame/
        Buffer.h; the mark stack is in Buffer.new, unbuilt). Enough for one
        flat buffer poured in order. Not enough for a tree of runs (position
        is run + offset), whole-paragraph breaking (decides after seeing the
        paragraph), re-flow on resize (needs each frame's START), or a frame
        read twice. Proposal: a pour cursor (run, offset) plus a BREAK
        RECORD -- where each frame's text begins and ends -- as the pour's
        output, kept out of the source tree. The Buffer mark stays as the
        leaf (a position inside one run). Old GUI's source= / next= is the
        same cursor at record grain, with listeners (A2).
        Parent copy plus named change: matches old GUI (Details setStyle);
        contradicted the WORDING of StreamModel ("not a scope tree").
        Reconciled by phase -- resolve as a tree in the pre-pass, paint as a
        forward-only stream. Now ruled: DesignDocs DisplayDesignHTML /
        StreamModel / StyleResolution. Build with lazy resolution (pointer
        to parent style + delta) and := / copyOf, never = (bear-traps 1, 2).
        Meaning-to-look lookup walks the enclosing frames like findAttribute;
        keep it separate from the copy.
        Additions: a third kind for the flow/field test -- a fixed box carried
        BY the flow (inline image, figure, unsplittable row); forms' space is
        stretch-only equal-weight glue, so move forms to the same glue model
        when typesetting adopts it (one mechanism); measure once per run in
        the pre-pass, through the one measure method the Display ruling
        reserves; frames name their successor (old GUI next=, text=Name);
        read Bot's PDF renderer before designing the backend (formsRecon,
        PDF recon: its TextRect "fill this frame, say how much you used" is
        the pour primitive); defer hyphenation, widows / orphans, floats.

